\meta{release}{Unreleased}
\meta{commit}{a3b189e}
\import{base-macros}

\p{With mapped input and \code{\verb~|-X scan-threads=N~} the reader's scanner threads now do part of the loader's work. They put a header in front of every stretch of consecutive application, lambda, Pi and let records that the exporter's layout marks as the proof of a theorem and prepare its packed form, and they resolve references to earlier records whose location is already final, so that two thirds of the references from proofs to earlier records never reach the loader thread (Std: 4,541,733 of about 5.8 M). On Mathlib 77,143,904 of 107,037,712 expression records go through packed runs. The store is identical with the one built before in all 14 reader configurations of the digest.}

\p{The load alone, warm on the JVM in quiet windows: Std 0.92 s with one thread against 0.48 to 0.56 s with two scanner threads and packing, cedar 1.36 against 0.73 s, Mathlib 9.2 to 9.6 s against 5.8 to 6.2 s, which meets the target of 7 s. A warm pass with 8 workers on 8 cores is not shorter (Std 1.64 to 1.68 s against 1.61 to 1.67 s before, Mathlib 21.05 against 20.56 s on the JVM): such a pass is at its CPU bound, and the total CPU of the load did not fall.}

\p{Detail: \src{rlvm-load/README.md}, \src{rlvm-export/README.md}. Commit \commit{a3b189e}. Session: [profiles and prototypes](ses-JRSD). Related: [the decision to go](dec-OC1F), [what packing gains and does not gain](ins-3L35).}
